  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday\nAssessment...high (>80%) confidence on cooler than normal\ntemperatures.\n\nA slightly stronger upper level disturbance will arrive in the \nFriday/Saturday time frame bringing a risk for rain. However, the \nsystem will be fighting relatively dry easterly flow ahead of it. \nAdd to this a surface based rex block from the strong Canadian high \nand the nor'easter on the east coast should result in a slower \narrival of the system.\n\nIndeed trends with the global models do appear to suggest a slightly \nnegative delta on the rain chances for Friday. Thus the Friday daylight\nhours and potentially evening hours may end up being mainly dry\nfor most of the area. The better timing for any rain appears to\nbe Saturday into Saturday night as the Canadian high moves \nfurther east and the better forcing from the system arrives. \nUnlike last week, rainfall amounts for this event will be on the\nlight side.\n\nThe next chance for rain is starting to center around \nTuesday/Wednesday of next week. During this time frame the models \nare showing a strong tropical connection to the systems in the \neastern Pacific up into the Midwest. The model consensus already has \na medium to high (50-70%) probability of rain occurring during \nthis time frame. PWATS of 1.5 to 2 inches are already being \nprogged by the global models indicating a high (>80%) \nprobability of heavy rain occurring.\n\nAlthough beyond the scope of the 7 day forecast, the high PWATs look \nto reload again late next week for the next system. This signal \nwould be consistent the with MJO vacillating between phases 8 and 1 \nwhich correlates to above normal precipitation for the area.",
